ve to a "C-square" adjacent to the empty corner A1. If you play here, you place a piece at B2. The opponent can then play A1, sandwiching your new piece at B2 against your existing piece at C3 (A1 -> B2 -> C3). This gives the opponent a corner, which is a major strategic blunder.\n2.  **(G, 2)**: Similarly, this is a C-square adjacent to the empty corner H1. If you play G2, the opponent can immediately play H1, sandwiching G2 against F3 (H1 -> G2 -> F3). This gives the opponent the H1 corner.\n3.  **(H, 2)**: This is a C-square on the edge. While it does not immediately grant the opponent the H1 corner (due to lack of a diagonal anchor for the opponent), playing next to an empty corner on the edge is generally structurally weak and limits your mobility while potentially aiding the opponent\'s edge play.\n4.  **(D, 7)**: This is an "internal" or central move. It flips the piece at E6. It is not adjacent to an empty corner and does not open up dangerous opportunities for the opponent. It fills a gap in the board structure safely.\n\nReason: Moves (B, 2) and (G, 2) are terrible because they immediately allow the opponent to capture corners A1 and H1. (H, 2) is positionally weak. (D, 7) is a safe, stable move that maintains control without giving up strategic advantages.\n\nAnswer: (D, 7)'}
